C = [
("h1", "3. Amos: Bethel, Gilgal and the Feast Days"),
("p", "Claim: Amos is the sharpest of the prophets on feasts and offerings, and he speaks to the northern kingdom at the sanctuaries its king had set up. His charge is double. The altar at Bethel and the feast Jeroboam devised were not the worship the LORD commanded, and the people who kept the feasts and brought the offerings, wherever they brought them, trampled the poor. His remedy is to seek the LORD and let justice run down like waters, and he gives no sentence that the order the LORD gave Israel was a mistake."),
("sub", "3.1 A man of Tekoa speaking to the north"),
("p", "The book opens with the man and the place. “The words of Amos, who was among the herdmen of Tekoa, which he saw concerning Israel in the days of Uzziah king of Judah, and in the days of Jeroboam the son of Joash king of Israel, two years before the earthquake” (Amos 1:1). Tekoa is in Judah and the king named first is Judah’s. The subject is Israel, the northern kingdom. The first thing Amos says in the LORD’s name points south: “The LORD will roar from Zion, and utter his voice from Jerusalem” (Amos 1:2). The prophet sends his word from Jerusalem to the north. That direction matters for what follows, because the sanctuary he goes against is not Jerusalem’s."),
("p", "The origin of the northern sanctuaries is given in the First Book of Kings. Jeroboam feared that if the people went up “to do sacrifice in the house of the LORD at Jerusalem,” their hearts would turn back to Rehoboam (1 Kings 12:27). So the king “made two calves of gold,” saying, “It is too much for you to go up to Jerusalem” (1 Kings 12:28). He set one in Bethel and the other in Dan (1 Kings 12:29). He “made an house of high places, and made priests of the lowest of the people, which were not of the sons of Levi” (1 Kings 12:31). He “ordained a feast in the eighth month, on the fifteenth day of the month, like unto the feast that is in Judah” and offered upon the altar at Bethel, “even in the month which he had devised of his own heart” (1 Kings 12:32, 33). Every element of the northern worship is named as the king’s own: the place, the calves, the priests, the month. The text says in so many words that the month was devised of his own heart."),
("p", "Amos meets the result in the last chapters of the book. Amaziah, the priest of Bethel, tells him, “prophesy not again any more at Bethel: for it is the king’s chapel, and it is the king’s court” (Amos 7:13). The priest’s own words say what Bethel was: the king’s chapel. The LORD had foretold it: “I will also visit the altars of Bethel: and the horns of the altar shall be cut off, and fall to the ground” (Amos 3:14). The volume reads the altars of Bethel as the altars Jeroboam made, not as the altar at Jerusalem, and the prophet’s sentence against them is a sentence against a worship the LORD did not command."),
("sub", "3.2 Come to Bethel, and transgress"),
("p", "Amos 4:4, 5 is the prophet’s irony. “Come to Bethel, and transgress; at Gilgal multiply transgression; and bring your sacrifices every morning, and your tithes after three years: and offer a sacrifice of thanksgiving with leaven, and proclaim and publish the free offerings: for this liketh you, O ye children of Israel, saith the Lord GOD.” The commands are in the form of a call to worship and mean the opposite. At Bethel, the call is to transgress. At Gilgal, to multiply transgression. The sacrifices, the tithes, the thank-offering and the free offerings are real, and the people bring them with zeal, every morning and on the third year. The reason is in the last clause: “for this liketh you.” The worship is what pleases the worshipers."),
("p", "The next verses show what the LORD did while they offered. “And I also have given you cleanness of teeth in all your cities, and want of bread in all your places: yet have ye not returned unto me, saith the LORD” (Amos 4:6). The sentence is repeated across the chapter as a refrain of judgments that did not turn them. The offering was brought and the LORD was not returned to. Verse 12 closes the chapter: “prepare to meet thy God, O Israel.” The offering at the wrong altar and the return of the heart are set side by side, and the first is no substitute for the second."),
("sub", "3.3 Seek me, and not Bethel"),
("p", "Chapter 5 turns the irony into command. “For thus saith the LORD unto the house of Israel, Seek ye me, and ye shall live: but seek not Bethel, nor enter into Gilgal, and pass not to Beersheba: for Gilgal shall surely go into captivity, and Bethel shall come to nought” (Amos 5:4, 5). The LORD names the places he does not want sought and sets himself in their place. The sentence is not a command to stop worshiping. It is a command to seek him, and it names three sanctuaries of the north and the south of the divided country, Bethel, Gilgal and Beersheba, as the places that are not the seeking. The same call is repeated: “Seek the LORD, and ye shall live; lest he break out like fire in the house of Joseph” (Amos 5:6)."),
("p", "The charge that goes with it is of conduct. The people “turn judgment to wormwood, and leave off righteousness in the earth” (Amos 5:7). They “hate him that rebuketh in the gate” (Amos 5:10). Their “treading is upon the poor,” and they take “burdens of wheat” from him (Amos 5:11). They “afflict the just, they take a bribe, and they turn aside the poor in the gate from their right” (Amos 5:12). The remedy is stated twice: “Seek good, and not evil, that ye may live: and so the LORD, the God of hosts, shall be with you, as ye have spoken” (Amos 5:14), and “Hate the evil, and love the good, and establish judgment in the gate: it may be that the LORD God of hosts will be gracious unto the remnant of Joseph” (Amos 5:15)."),
("sub", "3.4 I hate, I despise your feast days"),
("p", "Verses 18 to 20 speak to those who desire the day of the LORD and are told it is darkness, not light. Immediately after comes the most quoted sentence of the prophet. “I hate, I despise your feast days, and I will not smell in your solemn assemblies. Though ye offer me burnt offerings and your meat offerings, I will not accept them: neither will I regard the peace offerings of your fat beasts. Take thou away from me the noise of thy songs; for I will not hear the melody of thy viols. But let judgment run down as waters, and righteousness as a mighty stream” (Amos 5:21–24)."),
("p", "The list is complete: feast days, solemn assemblies, burnt offerings, meat offerings, peace offerings, songs and viols. Most are named with the pronoun: “your” feast days, “your” meat offerings, “your” fat beasts. The pronoun does the work in the same way as in Isaiah’s “your appointed feasts.” These are the feasts as this people keep them. The LORD does not say that burnt offerings are a sin. He says that he will not accept them from these hands, and that what he wants instead is a flow: “let judgment run down as waters, and righteousness as a mighty stream.” A stream is a thing that keeps running. The prophet asks that justice be as constant in Israel as the water in a river, and he sets that constancy against the constancy of the feasts."),
("p", "The Sabbath and the new moon appear again in chapter 8. Amos reports the speech of the merchants. “When will the new moon be gone, that we may sell corn? and the sabbath, that we may set forth wheat, making the ephah small, and the shekel great, and falsifying the balances by deceit? That we may buy the poor for silver, and the needy for a pair of shoes” (Amos 8:5, 6). The merchants keep the new moon and the sabbath, and they do not like it. They count the hours until they can cheat again. The days are observed, and the heart is elsewhere. The sentence is a charge against the dealers, and it takes the days of rest for granted as days the people kept. The Sabbath is not argued here; its place in the Law and in the prophets is left to Study 78."),
("sub", "3.5 Have ye offered unto me sacrifices in the wilderness?"),
("p", "The question of Amos 5:25 is the one most often quoted to show that the LORD never wanted sacrifices. “Have ye offered unto me sacrifices and offerings in the wilderness forty years, O house of Israel? But ye have borne the tabernacle of your Moloch and Chiun your images, the star of your god, which ye made to yourselves. Therefore will I cause you to go into captivity beyond Damascus, saith the LORD, whose name is The God of hosts” (Amos 5:25–27)."),
("p", "The question has the form of a rhetorical question, and readers differ on the answer it expects. The volume reads it with the verse that follows, which says that the people carried the tabernacle of Moloch and the images of their own star-god in the wilderness. The question does not ask whether the sacrifices were commanded in the wilderness. It asks whether the offerings were offered “unto me,” to the LORD, by a people who carried other gods in the same camp. The sentence pairs the offering with the idols, and the pairing is the charge. The captivity “beyond Damascus” is the sentence on the whole worship, the offerings and the idols together, and the sentence falls on the worshipers."),
("p", "Stephen quotes Amos 5:25–27 in Acts 7:42, 43 in a speech to the council, and the use is read in Acts Commentary Volume 1; this volume does not repeat it. What Amos himself says is enough for the present argument. He puts the wilderness sacrifices in question because of the idols that went with them. He does not say that the Law’s order was a mistake, and he does not say the offerings had been forbidden."),
("ctbl", "Amos in six passages.", [
    ("Passage", "What the LORD says against it", "What stands"),
    ("Amos 3:14; 7:13", "The altars of Bethel are to be cut off; Bethel is the king’s chapel", "The altar at Jerusalem is not the altar charged; the voice goes out from Zion (1:2)"),
    ("Amos 4:4, 5", "Come to Bethel and transgress; the sacrifices and free offerings are what “liketh you”", "The offerings are real and zealous; the LORD’s refrain is that they have not returned to him (4:6)"),
    ("Amos 5:4–6", "Seek not Bethel, Gilgal or Beersheba", "Seek ye me, and ye shall live"),
    ("Amos 5:21–24", "I hate, I despise your feast days; I will not accept your burnt offerings", "Let judgment run down as waters; the pronoun “your” limits the charge to how they keep them"),
    ("Amos 8:5, 6", "The merchants keep the new moon and the sabbath and count the hours until they can cheat", "The days are taken for granted as days the people kept"),
    ("Amos 5:25–27", "Did ye offer unto me in the wilderness, bearing Moloch and Chiun?", "The charge is the idols beside the offerings; the captivity falls on the worshipers"),
  ], [0.2, 0.42, 0.38]),
("sub", "3.6 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Amos speaks to the northern kingdom, from Tekoa, with the LORD roaring from Zion.",
    "The altar at Bethel, the calves, the priests and the month were the king’s own devising (1 Kings 12:26–33); Amos’s sentence against Bethel is a sentence against a worship the LORD did not command.",
    "The feasts and offerings of Amos 5:21–23 are named as “your” feasts and “your” offerings, in the hands of those who afflict the just and take a bribe.",
    "The remedy is to seek the LORD and to establish judgment in the gate; nothing in the book commands an end to the order the LORD gave Israel.",
    "Amos 5:25–27 pairs the wilderness offerings with the idols carried beside them, and the charge falls on the people who brought both.",
  ]),
]
